            <ArticleTitle>[The effect of long-term Enduracin monotherapy on the clinical and biochemical status of patients with ischemic heart disease].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>41-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>13 patients aged 39 to 60 years with coronary atherosclerosis confirmed at selective coronary angiography combined with primary hyperlipidemia (phenotypes 2a and 2b) received enduracin in a dose 1500 mg/day. As a result of the treatment total cholesterol (TC) and LDL cholesterol lowered by 10.3 and 13.1%, respectively, whereas HDL cholesterol rose by 15.2%. Half of the patients demonstrated activation of hepatic transaminases, but discontinuation of the drug was not necessary. In 3 out of 4 patients after 2 years of enduracin treatment stabilization of atherosclerosis was observed. Thus, long-term enduracin is able to inhibit progression of atherosclerosis in coronary heart disease patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
